#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/id #uuid "4b2fcf5d-0da3-5676-b4c6-54095c3fb45c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5486" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 - λ & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 - λ & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-9 - λ & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 - λ & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-9 - λ)(9 - λ)(6 - λ) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 9)(λ - 9)(λ - 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-9\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124250 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "76c52a74-9b98-5df5-ac63-64e92af1a580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5487" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}1 - λ & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix}- (-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix}+ (-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 - λ \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(- λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} + 17λ - 11) + (3 + λ) - 2(4 - 2λ) &= 0 \\\\\n- λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} + 22λ - 16 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{3} + 5λ^{2} - 22λ + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16$:\n$\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16$\n\nLet's pick $x=1$. Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1)^{3} + 5(1)^{2} - 22(1) + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_2=2,\\,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_3=-8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/id #uuid "a1c51187-c0a6-505c-bc58-2319b439086e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7047" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n2. $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n3. The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}3 - λ & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(P - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 - λ & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 - λ) \\cdot (4 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 - λ & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)(4 - λ)((-1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 35) &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)(4 - λ)(λ^{2} - 36) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 3)(λ - 4)(λ - 6)(λ + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3$, $\\lambda=4$, $\\lambda=6$, and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa0ef8ff-9fec-507b-862c-a9f0e17b6225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + λ - 16) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122621} {:answer/id #uuid "0394eed9-f552-5dad-a903-5c47d8323de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + λ + 4) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122622} {:answer/id #uuid "19ff8716-e2a6-5629-80f6-c6807a9f8e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + λ - 4) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122623} {:answer/id #uuid "4c044c6d-cef5-5628-b2d4-aeb11b1848ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + λ - 10) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122624} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2f700f-2d6f-5fe4-98de-ef176065458c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - λ)(λ^{2} - 16) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0ef8ff-9fec-507b-862c-a9f0e17b6225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + λ - 16) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122621} :answer-field/id #uuid "513cd4e0-dd21-5dc0-8816-a326879209bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122620}] :question/id #uuid "2accab64-f6f1-5300-b0aa-a3471aee3ef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-36845" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d868ab84-6de3-56db-8f3c-ac4fdb47c961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122891} {:answer/id #uuid "13c64a8c-b57a-5048-8c6a-78db148f5492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122892} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb5f3ce-8662-5133-851b-929be999c756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122893} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe66905-f33a-5d2c-b84c-24cabf303a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122894} {:answer/id #uuid "48b715ce-7730-5b5d-bffb-669208dbf7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c64a8c-b57a-5048-8c6a-78db148f5492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122892} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab1bc0ca-1fdb-57f2-9a7b-c33a2e97dc5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122890}] :question/id #uuid "c699a77c-6393-5572-b010-6743d9f3bb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-37355" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $λ =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n2. $λ =-10$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n3. The eigenvalue $λ =-10$ has algebraic multiplicity $3$"}]}
